[38;5;231m⏺[39m Reminders it is. One important constraint to state plainly before the next question, because it bounds what's honestly
  deliverable:

  [1mA static client-side app can only fire notifications while it's running.[0m No server means no push. A service worker can
  survive tab closure somewhat, but without a push backend the browser will not reliably wake it to fire a scheduled
  reminder — and the Notification Triggers API that would have done this never shipped broadly. So "remind me at 9am
  tomorrow while the app is closed" is not achievable in this architecture. What [3mis[0m achievable: reminders fire while the
  app is open, and overdue items are surfaced on next open.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how a due reminder reaches the user.

  - [1mIn-page only[0m — a notifications panel/badge in the UI, plus toasts. No permission prompt, no new APIs, works
    everywhere, testable without browser mocks. Costs: invisible unless the app is in front of you.
  - [1mIn-page + OS notifications[0m — same, plus [38;5;153mNotification[39m API so reminders surface outside the tab while the app is open
    in a background tab. Costs: a permission prompt, a permission-denied path to design, and harder testing. Cheap to
    add later on top of in-page — the reverse is not true.
  - [1mService worker[0m — adds offline shell and some background capability. Costs real complexity for a benefit that,
    without push, mostly doesn't materialize here.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m in-page first, with the delivery layer behind a small interface so OS notifications are an additive
  change rather than a rewrite. I'd skip the service worker entirely — YAGNI given no push backend.
